          <h3 class="slide-title">4. Phonological Foundations & Word Structure</h3>
          <span class="std-badge">MN 7.1.4.4</span>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-body">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Phonemes:</strong> The smallest individual units of spoken sound in a language (e.g., the 3 distinct sounds in /k/ /a/ /t/).</li>
            <li><strong>Graphemes:</strong> The written letters or combinations of letters that represent spoken phonemes.</li>
            <li><strong>Phonological Awareness:</strong> The ability to recognize, isolate, and manipulate spoken sound structures before reading written words.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <strong style="color: var(--primary-dark);">💡 Key Insight:</strong> Strong readers use sound-pattern recognition to automatically decode complex academic vocabulary.
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Slide 5 -->
      <div class="slide" id="slide-5">
        <div class="slide-header">
          <h3 class="slide-title">5. Morphological Analysis: Roots & Affixes</h3>
          <span class="std-badge">MN 7.1.4.4</span>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-body">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Morpheme:</strong> The smallest unit of language that carries distinct meaning.</li>
            <li><strong>Root/Base Word:</strong> The core element containing the primary meaning (e.g., <em>struct</em> = to build).</li>
            <li><strong>Prefixes:</strong> Morphemes added to the beginning of a root (e.g., <em>de-</em>, <em>re-</em>, <em>con-</em>).</li>
            <li><strong>Suffixes:</strong> Morphemes added to the end of a root (e.g., <em>-tion</em>, <em>-ive</em>, <em>-ture</em>).</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <strong style="color: var(--primary-dark);">🔍 Deconstruction Strategy:</strong> Strip affixes away from unfamiliar words to isolate the core meaning of the root.
        </div>
      </div>

